## Two things in the recipe tree are not ours

Generated recipes -- `recipes/*.sh` whose header says CANDIDATE -- are derived from the
Linux From Scratch and Beyond Linux From Scratch books, which are not one license but
two.

    Linux From Scratch, version 13.0-systemd
    Copyright (C) 1999-2026 Gerard Beekmans
    https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/lfs/

    Beyond Linux From Scratch (systemd edition), version 13.0
    Copyright (C) 1999-2026 The BLFS Development Team
    https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/blfs/

**The command blocks are MIT, and that is fine.** Each book's legal notice says
"computer instructions may be extracted from the book under the MIT License." MIT is a
GPL-compatible license, so extracted commands can be combined with GPL work and
redistributed under the GPL without difficulty. Keep the attribution: every generated
recipe names its source page in its own header.

**The quoted book prose is CC BY-NC-SA 2.0, and that is a genuine problem.** Each
generated recipe carries book text verbatim in two places:

    # title  : <the book page's own section title>
    #   ctx:  <the paragraph surrounding each command block>

Around 2,800 such lines. They exist because a command extracted from a book without the
prose that qualified it cannot be reviewed, and reviewing it against the book's own words
is the entire point of the override mechanism.

That content is outside the MIT extraction permission, and its NonCommercial clause is
**not GPL-compatible and not a free software license**. The FSF is explicit that
NonCommercial licenses fail the freedoms, and the GPL forbids imposing restrictions beyond
its own terms, so CC BY-NC-SA content cannot form part of a combined GPL work.

### Why the repository is still coherent, and where the line is

The GPL programs in `bin/` do not incorporate that prose. They read the books from
`book/` -- which is not distributed here at all -- and emit it at runtime. Program output
is not covered by the GPL, and a distribution medium carrying separate independent works
alongside a GPL work is an aggregate, not a combined work (GPLv3 section 5). On that
reading the GPL is satisfied: nothing in `bin/` is encumbered, and no GPL term is
violated by the recipes sitting next to it.

What remains true regardless of that reading:

  - The generated recipe files carry the books' terms, not ours. Redistributing them
    means honoring **Attribution**, **NonCommercial** and **ShareAlike**.
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/2.0/
  - So the repository **as distributed** contains non-free content, and cannot be used
    commercially as a whole. That is a real limitation, and it is exactly what the FSF
    advises against shipping.

### Removing it entirely

The generated recipes are reproducible artifacts, not source. Everything needed to
recreate them is here and is GPL: the package lists in `packages/` and
`hosts/*/packages.py`, and every review decision in the `*-overrides.json` files. The
books themselves are fetched, not vendored.

Stop tracking `recipes/*.sh` for CANDIDATE steps, and this repository becomes
GPL-3.0-or-later throughout with no non-free content in it. A clone then runs

    bin/extract-recipes.py && bin/extract-blfs.py

to produce them locally, which `--check` already proves is faithful: it re-derives every
recipe from the book plus the recorded decisions and reports any that differ. Nothing is
lost except the ability to read a recipe without fetching the book first.

## Other third-party content

Two upstream patches are tracked because they are build inputs the BLFS mirrors do not
version, and they carry their own upstream terms:

    blfs-staging/ffmpeg-8.0.1-chromium_method-1.patch
    blfs-staging/node-v22.22.0-python_build_fix-1.patch

`recipes/blfs-firefox.sh` contains a Google API key. It is BLFS's own published key,
reproduced verbatim from that book page, not a credential of this project. See the
comment above it in the recipe.

Upstream source tarballs and the books are not vendored. `blfs-staging/urls.txt` and
`blfs-staging/blfs-md5` record what to fetch and what it must hash to.
